"# Chapter 6 — The World Loop

Painting one picture is a still. Painting sixty pictures per second is a movie. This chapter teaches you the shape Motoi uses to run a movie: an explicit loop that carries a state, advances it, draws it, and checks whether to stop. It is the shape every real-time program passes through, from a bouncing ball to a full game.

The pattern comes from How to Design Programs — a teaching classic — and it survives untouched in Motoi because it is honest about what a game loop actually is: a state, a way to advance the state, a way to draw the state, and a way to stop. We do not hide it behind a magic verb. We write the loop ourselves, out of a named recursion you already met in Chapter 4.

## Why the loop is just a named recursion

A game loop is boring plumbing. Every game has the same shape at the outermost layer: on each frame, compute the new state; then draw the current state; then check whether the game is over. The interesting logic sits inside those three slots. The plumbing is the same every time.

You already have everything you need to write that plumbing: a named `let loop`, or a recursive function that calls itself with the next state. The state threads through the loop like a river — each frame you scoop up the old state, produce a new state, draw it, and hand the new state back to the loop.

There is a second reason this shape is worth reaching for. Because each step is a pure function of state — and only the state carries information from frame to frame — you can time-travel debug. Save the state at any frame. Reload it. Replay from there. The composition doctrine you saw in Book of Composition Chapter 1 (round-trip: file and tool are the same) applies here too: state and game are the same thing.

## Problem 1 — A counter that ticks up

The smallest useful loop: a number that goes up one per frame, and a display that shows it. We write `run` as a named recursion. It takes the state and the frame number; each call advances both; it stops at a frame bound.

```
(define (advance state) (+ state 1))

(define (draw state)
  (begin-frame 'main)
  (text/draw (number->string state) 150 110 'pink 'big)
  (end-frame 'main))

(define (run state frame stop)
  (draw state)
  (if (= frame stop)
      state
      (run (advance state) (+ frame 1) stop)))

(run 0 0 300)
;; => 300   (the loop ran 300 frames; final state is 300)
```

Read the three pieces. `advance` receives the current state and returns the next — here just `state + 1`. `draw` receives the current state and paints it — one big pink number in the middle. `run` is the loop: draw, then either stop or recurse with the advanced state and the next frame number.

That is a whole real-time program. It counts from 0 to 300. Change `advance` to add `2` and it counts twice as fast. Change the `stop` argument to `1000000` and it counts (nearly) forever.

## Problem 2 — A bouncing ball

Now the state is a body, not a number. A body is a plain value: a `(pos vel)` pair, where `pos` and `vel` are vectors. We advance it with `vec/add` and flip a velocity component when the ball hits an edge.

```
(define (make-body pos vel) (list pos vel))
(define (body-pos b) (car b))
(define (body-vel b) (cadr b))

(define (advance b)
  (let* ((p  (body-pos b))
         (v  (body-vel b))
         (np (vec/add p v))
         (nx (car np))
         (ny (cadr np))
         (vx (if (or (< nx 0) (> nx 320)) (- (car v))  (car v)))
         (vy (if (or (< ny 0) (> ny 240)) (- (cadr v)) (cadr v))))
    (make-body np (vec/make vx vy))))

(define (draw b)
  (let ((p (body-pos b)))
    (begin-frame 'main)
    (text/draw \"O\" (car p) (cadr p) 'pink 'big)
    (end-frame 'main)))

(define (run b frame stop)
  (draw b)
  (if (= frame stop) b (run (advance b) (+ frame 1) stop)))

(run (make-body (vec/make 100 100) (vec/make 3 2)) 0 120)
;; => ((184 144) (-3 -2))   (after 120 frames it has bounced off both edges)
```

The state is the body. `advance` computes the new state — position plus velocity via `vec/add`, and a velocity component flips when the ball would leave the screen. `draw` paints a marker at the body's position. `run` threads the body through the loop.

Two design choices worth noticing.

First: we made accessors (`body-pos`, `body-vel`) so the rest of the code never asks `(car b)` or `(cadr b)` by hand. If tomorrow a body gains a color, only the constructor and one new accessor change. Everywhere else keeps working.

Second: `advance` and `draw` each receive a bare state and produce a value. Neither mutates. The body that comes out of `advance` is a brand-new pair; the old one is untouched. This is why time-travel debugging is possible: the state is the whole story, and every past state still exists.

## Problem 3 — A ball under gravity, using phys/kinematics

Instead of writing the physics by hand, hand it to Motoi. `phys/kinematics` integrates one axis: given a starting position, starting velocity, acceleration, and a time step, it returns the new `(position velocity)`.

```
(phys/kinematics 100 5 -9.8 1)
;; => (100.1 -4.800000000000001)
```

Read it: start at position `100` moving up at `5`, under gravity `-9.8`, after `1` time unit you are at `100.1` and now moving *down* at `-4.8`. Gravity turned the ball around.

Wrap that in the same loop shape. The state is a `(y vy)` pair for the vertical axis.

```
(define (advance-y state)
  (phys/kinematics (car state) (cadr state) -9.8 0.1))

(define (draw-y state)
  (begin-frame 'main)
  (text/draw \"o\" 160 (car state) 'peach 'default)
  (end-frame 'main))

(define (run state frame stop)
  (draw-y state)
  (if (= frame stop) state (run (advance-y state) (+ frame 1) stop)))

(run (list 20 0) 0 30)
;; => a (y vy) pair after 30 steps — the ball has fallen and sped up
```

You wrote zero integration code. `phys/kinematics` did the calculus. Each frame it takes the old `(y vy)`, applies gravity for one small time step, and hands back the new `(y vy)`. The loop is exactly the same shape as Problem 2 — only `advance` changed.

## Problem 4 (pro) — Many bodies at once

The loop does not care whether the state is one body or a hundred. Make the state a *list* of bodies and step them all with `map`.

```
(define (make-body pos vel) (list pos vel))
(define (body-pos b) (car b))
(define (body-vel b) (cadr b))

(define (step-body b)
  (make-body (vec/add (body-pos b) (body-vel b)) (body-vel b)))

(define (advance bodies)
  (map step-body bodies))

(define (draw bodies)
  (begin-frame 'main)
  (for-each
    (lambda (b)
      (let ((p (body-pos b)))
        (text/draw \"*\" (car p) (cadr p) 'yellow 'tiny)))
    bodies)
  (end-frame 'main))

(define (spawn-row count y vx)
  (map (lambda (i) (make-body (vec/make (* i 30) y) (vec/make vx 0)))
       (iota count)))

(define (run bodies frame stop)
  (draw bodies)
  (if (= frame stop) bodies (run (advance bodies) (+ frame 1) stop)))

(run (spawn-row 5 100 2) 0 10)
;; => a list of 5 bodies, each advanced 10 frames to the right
```

Read the pattern. `spawn-row` builds a list of bodies with `iota` (which returns `(0 1 2 ...)`) and `map`. `advance` steps *every* body with one `map`. `draw` paints every body with one `for-each`. The loop is unchanged — the state just happens to be a list now.

Five bodies, one `advance`, one `draw`. If you had done this by hand you would repeat the position update five times. `map` bundles it into one call. Change `5` to `50` and nothing else changes.

## Capstone — A pause flag that suspends `advance` without suspending the loop

Extend the state to a pair: `(bodies paused?)`. When paused, `advance` returns the bodies untouched; the loop keeps running and keeps drawing.

```
(define (make-world bodies paused?) (list bodies paused?))
(define (world-bodies w) (car w))
(define (world-paused? w) (cadr w))

(define (advance-world w)
  (if (world-paused? w)
      w
      (make-world (map step-body (world-bodies w)) #f)))

(define (toggle-pause w)
  (make-world (world-bodies w) (not (world-paused? w))))

(define (run w frame stop)
  (draw (world-bodies w))
  (if (= frame stop) w (run (advance-world w) (+ frame 1) stop)))

(run (make-world (spawn-row 3 120 1) #f) 0 5)
;; => a paused-flag world after 5 frames
```

We extended the state with a `paused?` flag. `advance-world` short-circuits the physics when paused but the loop still draws every frame. `toggle-pause` flips the flag — you would call it from a key handler.

Adding a feature by extending the state and short-circuiting a step — no hidden globals, no side channels — is the shape you want. It scales from three bodies to a large simulation without ever asking `where does the state live`, because the state lives exactly where the loop passes it.

